+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for competing visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers submit visit records from 12 shared tablets that can be offline for 48 hours. Two workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes, and both corrections can arrive days apart. The record store must define what "current" means when that happens, because the mechanism chosen here determines what tablets must store and send, what the coordinators' review work looks like, and which record states may enter the Friday CSV export to the regional science partner.
+
+A visit is a scientific unit: a species identification and its cover estimate, an observer list and a date, belong to one observation made by one worker. Edits to a visit can be unrelated (correcting an observer name; adding a missing species) or scientifically related (revising an identification and revising its cover estimate together). The server cannot infer these relationships from field names.
+
+What's at stake: a mechanism that combines edits automatically can assemble a record no worker actually made and export it as if someone had; a mechanism that routes every conflict to review costs coordinator time on a team with exactly two coordinators and about 6,000 visits per season. Either way, original submissions must survive untouched — that is a standing tenet and a background constraint, not a discriminator between the options.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Whole-visit scientific integrity** (major) — an exported visit should be a record a specific worker authored as a unit. A server that combines field edits across authors can produce a record nobody wrote.
+- **Failure visibility** (major) — when the mechanism errs, does the error surface in the review queue or silently in the export? Turns on the tenets "make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export" and "do not silently discard a submitted edit."
+- **Path inspectability** (major) — every exported value traces to an authored submission. Turns on the tenet "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable."
+- **Review workload** (major) — coordinator attention is the scarcest operational resource: two coordinators against seasonal volume.
+- **Maintained rules and machinery** (moderate) — anything the mechanism must "know" about fields (scalar rules, list-merge rules, species-entry rules) is code that must be written, kept current as fields evolve, and trusted at export time.
+- **Tablet-side complexity** (moderate) — what the offline client must retain and transmit to participate in the mechanism.
+- **Submission size** (minor) — payload bytes per edit; visit payloads are small at this scale.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description**: Each edit is submitted as a complete visit payload naming the revision it was made against (its base revision). A submission whose base is the visit's current revision becomes the new current record. A submission whose base is anything else is held as an unresolved candidate and the visit enters review; while unresolved, further submissions are held as candidates too. A coordinator resolves by adopting one candidate or affirming the current record; unselected revisions are retained as superseded, never deleted. The server performs no merging of any kind.
+
+**Demonstration**: Worker A corrects the observer name on visit V based on revision 4; worker B adds a missing species to V, also based on revision 4. The store holds revision 5 (A's complete visit) and revision 6 (B's complete visit) as candidates; the current record stays revision 4 until a coordinator adopts one — or submits their own correction based on whichever they adopt.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **strong** — every stored and exported state is one worker's complete visit.
+- Failure visibility: **strong** — the mechanism's only failure mode is a growing review queue, which coordinators see; it cannot mis-combine.
+- Path inspectability: **strong** — each exported value names one revision, one author, one receipt time.
+- Review workload: **weak** — even unrelated-field edits from two workers create a full review item.
+- Maintained rules and machinery: **strong** — no merge rules exist; conflict detection is a single revision-ID comparison.
+- Tablet-side complexity: **strong** — the client retains a base revision ID per draft and sends whole payloads.
+- Submission size: **weak** — each edit re-sends the entire visit.
+
+**Summary**: Buys integrity, visibility, and simplicity by spending coordinator attention on every conflict.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description**: Each edit carries its base state and structured changes. The server maintains merge rules per field type: scalar fields take the single change; list fields such as the observer list and repeated entries such as species records follow element-level combination rules. Changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new record; overlapping changes to the same field are routed to review with field-level provenance.
+
+**Demonstration**: On visit V (base revision 4), A changes the observer name and B adds a species. The rules see disjoint fields and automatically produce revision 5 containing both changes, attributed to both submissions; a later edit touching the same species record would instead raise an overlap for review.
+
+**Analysis**:
+- Whole-visit scientific integrity: **weak** — the auto-combined record is the server's composition, not any worker's submission; rules cannot see that an identification change and a cover-estimate change belong together.
+- Failure visibility: **weak** — a wrong combination, crossing scientifically related fields the rules do not model, surfaces only if a coordinator happens to inspect the merged record; otherwise it exports silently.
+- Path inspectability: **partial** — field-level provenance machinery can say which edit contributed each value, but the record as a whole has no single author.
+- Review workload: **strong** — only genuinely overlapping edits reach review; most visits never do.
+- Maintained rules and machinery: **weak** — merge rules per field type are permanent maintenance, and their correctness is load-bearing for export.
+- Tablet-side complexity: **weak** — the client must retain base snapshots and emit structured changes rather than whole payloads.
+- Submission size: **strong** — edits transmit deltas, not full visits.
+
+**Summary**: Buys review volume and bandwidth by making rule maintenance and rule correctness load-bearing.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions. No gate eliminated the merge option; it loses on the weighting. The conflict rate that drives review volume is bounded by how often two workers correct the same visit independently — an exception in a season of 6,000 visits, not the rule — while the merge option's costs (rule maintenance, silent mis-combination of scientifically related edits) apply to every automated combination and fail silently exactly where the tenets are least tolerant. The decision turns on "do not silently discard a submitted edit," "keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable," and "make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export." The review-workload cost is taken knowingly, with two coordinators as the operating constraint.
+
+Committing forecloses server-side combination of edits in the first release, and obligates:
+
+- **Tablet-sync**: retain the base revision ID with each draft; transmit whole-visit payloads; present submission outcomes to the worker — current, held as a candidate, superseded — including that a held submission may sit unresolved behind others' work; size local storage for full payloads rather than deltas.
+- **Review-export**: provide a revision-comparison view over competing candidates; hold unresolved visits out of the Friday CSV; treat resolution as selection (adopt or affirm), with coordinator corrections entering as new submissions; keep per-revision attribution in export provenance rather than field-level merge lineage.
+- **Both**: link to the record-store contract (REC specs) rather than restating concurrency semantics.
+
+Revisit this decision if review volume proves unmanageable in practice — unresolved visits chronically delaying the Friday export, or coordinators unable to clear the queue within a season. The append-only revision store does not preclude a merge aid at that point: field-aware combination could be introduced as a review-time suggestion over stored revisions without changing store policy. What would not survive reversal is silent automatic combination feeding the export directly.
